{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## ROOT-CAUSE VERDICT: NOT RESOLVABLE IN REBAR — the payload is right, the field is the problem\n\nInvestigated read-only. The question was whether `\"data was not an object\"` meant DC refuses\nintrinsically, or meant **rebar sends the wrong payload** — the latter would have made this our bug.\nIt is the former.\n\n### What rebar actually sends, verified end to end\n\n`transport.py:736-737` (sub-task branch): `body = {\"parent\": None}` for a clear, passed to\n`issue.update(fields=body)`. Read the INSTALLED pycontribs source\n(`.venv/.../jira/resources.py`) rather than assuming: `Issue.update` puts `fields` into\n`data[\"fields\"]` **unmodified** and PUTs `json.dumps(data)`. No verb translation, no client-side\nmangling. DC therefore receives literally `{\"fields\":{\"parent\":null}}`.\n\n### Why DC rejects it\n\nJira's `parent` field deserializer requires an OBJECT (`{\"key\":…}` / `{\"id\":…}`) and rejects any\nother shape — `null` included — with exactly `{\"errors\":{\"parent\":\"data was not an object\"}}`. That\nis a TYPE-level failure that fires before any \"is a parent required\" business rule. Separately, a\nsub-task's parent is a mandatory part of the type on DC: the supported way to remove the\nrelationship is the **Move / issue-conversion** operation (which changes the issue's type away from\nsub-task), not a field PUT. `JRASERVER-34648` corroborates the hard invariant.\n\nThe `update`-verb form (`{\"update\":{\"parent\":[{\"set\":null}]}}`) would pass the identical\nobject-typed value through the same deserializer, and `parent` exposes only a `set` operation (it is\nnot a multi-valued field with add/remove). **Flagged honestly: that last point is inference from the\ndocumented operation set, not an experiment — no live DC instance was available.**\n\n### Verdict and confidence\n\n**NOT RESOLVABLE** as a rebar payload fix. HIGH confidence on what rebar sends (read directly, both\nsides). MEDIUM-HIGH that DC rejects every verb form — multiple independent reports of the identical\nerror string, but not verified against THIS instance.\n\n**Cheap falsifiers, worth folding into the next harness run since they cost almost nothing:**\n 1. `GET /rest/api/2/issue/{subtask}/editmeta` — if `parent` lists any operation besides `set`\n    (e.g. `remove`), an alternate clear exists and this verdict is wrong.\n 2. `PUT` the `update`-verb form and compare the error to the `fields` form. A DIFFERENT error would\n    mean the verb form matters.\n\n### Recommendation — option 1, plus option 2 as a cheap pin\n\n**Option 1 (use a NON-sub-task child and clear its Epic Link)** is the only option that restores\nREAL live coverage. It exercises `set_parent`'s non-sub-task branch — a genuinely nullable custom\nfield, not the type-locked `parent` system field — and it is the closer analog to production, where\nmost reconciled issues are not sub-tasks. It also composes with change 1311, which just built that\nbranch.\n\n**Option 2 (assert the refusal as the contract)** is rejected as the PRIMARY path — it proves only\nthat DC still refuses, which we already know, and gives zero positive assurance that a successful\nclear round-trips. But it is worth adding ALONGSIDE option 1 as a regression pin, so a future DC\nversion that starts accepting the null fails loudly and reopens the coverage question.\n\n**Option 3 (accept unit-only coverage)** rejected given this epic's record — changes 1276 and 1288\nwere both mutation-proven and both wrong in production. Fallback only if option 1 proves infeasible.\n\n### ESCALATED\n\nPer the operator's ruling (\"after running rebar-debug on the platform refusal, escalate to me if it\nis not resolvable\"), this is escalated. The decision needed: accept option 1 + 2 as the resolution\nof this ticket (restoring live coverage on a different, representable field), or treat the platform\nconstraint itself as closing it."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785792549842424001,"uuid":"deac7c4a-293b-4878-8412-138c0cfea2f0"}